  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...As of 2:30 AM EDT...Current sfc analysis shows \na 993 mb sfc low located just east of James Bay, with its \ntrailing cold front extending south across central and western \nNY. Radar imagery shows some isolated to scattered showers ahead\nof this front across the Mohawk Valley and southern ADKs. A \ncouple rumbles of thunder can't be ruled out with still some \npockets of elevated instability per latest SPC mesoanalysis, but\nno severe weather is expected through tonight. With light winds\nand mostly cloud skies, temperatures and dew points are mainly \nin the 60s across the region, and not expected to fall much from\ncurrent levels between now and sunrise. \n\nThis morning, the cold front tracks eastwards through the \nregion, although it will slow down as it reaches western New\nEngland. While the best upper forcing will remain northwest of\nthe region, low-level convergence along the front may be able to\nhelp initiate a few showers or thunderstorms over the Taconics\nor western New England by late morning into this afternoon.\nLatest HREF suggests 500 to maybe 1000 J/kg of SBCAPE ahead of\nthe cold front overlapping with 40-45 kt of deep-layer shear.\nHodographs are fairly long with some counter-clockwise curvature\nthanks to backing winds with height. This would generally be a \nrelatively favorable environment for severe weather with \nsplitting cells and even some left movers. However, abundant \nmid-level dry air and subsidence will be very difficult for any \nupdrafts to overcome and be able to sustain themselves. 700-500 \nmb lapse rates are also unimpressive, with a mid-level cap \ndeveloping this afternoon. So, these factors will help mitigate \nthe severe weather threat. SPC has clipped far southeastern \nBerkshire County and portions of Litchfield County with the \nmarginal risk for severe weather today, but the threat is very \nconditional on updrafts being able to overcome the mid-level \ndry air. \n\nWhile a few showers are possible across the ADKs today closer to\nthe upper trough and due to some westerly upslope enhancement,\nmost of the rest of the region should see a drying trend with\nlowering humidity through the day. Highs will mainly be in the\n70s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...All areas dry out tonight as sfc high pressure\nbuilds into the region from the west. The sfc high will remain\noverhead or just to our east through Saturday morning. During\nthis period, we will see comfortable humidity and quiet weather.\nDaytime highs Thursday will only be in the 70s with the upper\ntrough still overhead, but warm into the 70s to 80s Friday as\nheights rise aloft. Overnight lows will be quite comfortable\neach night with 40s to 50s. We went cooler than the NBM Thursday\nnight and Friday night with the sfc high overhead. Some patchy\nradiation fog will also be possible in the typical valley areas\neach night as well.\n\nSaturday into early next week, the sfc high slides off to our\neast, setting up S/SW return flow into our region. Temperatures\nrise slightly above normal into the mid 80s for valley areas\nSaturday through Monday. Dew points remain fairly comfortable,\nbut do begin to creep up as well especially Sunday into Monday.\nAnother approaching upper trough and diffluent flow aloft may\nlead to renewed shower chances late Sunday and early next week,\nbut none of these days currently look to be a washout.",
